/* Engelsk lärguide och ordlista (v9.0) – laddas bara i engelskt läge. */
window.GUIDE_EN = [{"title": "Module 1 — The basics", "lessons": [{"id": "vad-ar-aktie", "title": "What is a share and a share price?", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine ten friends who together own a pizzeria. If you split the ownership into 100 equal \"shares\", each of you owns 10 shares. A stock share is exactly the same thing, but in a company: a small, identical piece of ownership. The share price is simply what the market is currently willing to pay for one such piece.</p></div><p>When you buy a share you are not buying \"a lottery ticket\" or \"a receipt\" — you literally become a part-owner of the company, albeit a very small one in a large company. You are entitled to your share of future profits (often paid as dividends) and to vote at the annual general meeting, although with negligible influence if you own few shares.</p><p>The share price moves all the time because it is set by buyers and sellers meeting on an exchange — just as the price of a second-hand bike can vary depending on how many people want that particular model right now. If more people are willing to pay more to own a piece of the company, the price rises. If more want to sell than buy, it falls.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Market capitalisation</h3><p><strong>Market cap</strong> is the whole company's price tag: share price × number of shares outstanding. If the pizzeria has 100 shares and each costs 50 kr, the whole pizzeria is worth 5,000 kr on paper. Market cap determines, among other things, whether a company counts as large, mid-sized or small — something that comes up later in the guide and that the app takes into account.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>PRICE</td><td>Latest closing price, in the company's trading currency</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "poangmodellen-grund", "title": "How the score (0–100) works", "body": "<p>Every stock in the app gets a score between 0 and 100. It is not a truth about the future — it is a weighting of around twenty measurable factors, which you will learn one by one in this guide. The more positive signals that point in the same direction, the higher the score.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Think of a medical check-up. The doctor doesn't just look at blood pressure to judge how healthy you are — they weigh together blood pressure, pulse, blood tests, weight and many other measures. A single value rarely tells the whole truth, but together they paint a reasonable overall picture. The score in the app works the same way: it combines valuation, trend, quality and risk into a single number, but no single factor decides everything.</p></div><h3 class=\"sub\">Starting point: 50</h3><p>Every stock starts at <strong>50 points</strong> — a neutral position, neither good nor bad. Bonuses are then added for positive signals (cheap valuation, uptrend, strong growth) and deductions are made for negative ones (expensive valuation, downtrend, high debt). The total is clipped to between 0 and 100.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Diminishing returns on bonuses</h3><p>An important design choice: <strong>deductions always count in full</strong> — a genuinely bad stock should be able to end up near zero. But <strong>bonuses are dampened</strong> beyond a certain limit (25 points), so it takes an unusually large number of positive factors at once to reach scores near 100. This keeps the ceiling realistic instead of many stocks bunching up at 95–100.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>≥65 points</td><td>Flagged BUY (unless analyst consensus disagrees — more on that later)</td></tr><tr><td>≥40 sell score</td><td>Flagged SELL for holdings you own</td></tr><tr><td>&lt;40 points</td><td>Not shown as a candidate in the USA/Europe/Nordics tabs</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "navigera-appen", "title": "How to read the app's interface and symbols", "body": "<p>Before diving into the analysis itself, it is worth recognising the symbols that recur in the list, so you don't have to guess what they mean when you see them.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">The tabs</h3><p>The app has four main tabs: <strong>Macro</strong> (stability, rates, world situation, market breadth), <strong>Stocks</strong>, <strong>Funds</strong> and <strong>Guide</strong>. Under Stocks, <strong>USA</strong>, <strong>Europe</strong> and <strong>Nordics</strong> show buy candidates by market. <strong>Growth</strong> shows hand-picked young/small companies with their own, more risk-tolerant scoring model (more on that later). <strong>My holdings</strong> shows your own stocks, uploaded as a file that never leaves your browser — the same file also feeds <strong>Funds → My funds</strong>.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Symbols in the name row</h3><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">Symbol → meaning</div><table><tr><td>BUY / HOLD / SELL</td><td>Coloured flag based on score and analyst consensus together</td></tr><tr><td>★</td><td>New on the list — added in the last few days</td></tr><tr><td>3D, 5D etc.</td><td>Number of runs in a row with an active signal — an indication of how stable the signal has been, not just a one-off</td></tr><tr><td>+↑ / +↓</td><td>Golden cross / death cross, or imminent (within 2%)</td></tr><tr><td>↑20 / ↓20</td><td>Same idea but a faster, earlier warning signal</td></tr><tr><td>IT#N</td><td>Rank N on Investtech's external Top 20 list (technical analysis)</td></tr></table></div><p>If you open a stock (click the row, or the ⓘ button) you see a full breakdown of all key figures, a list of reasons showing exactly which factors pulled the score up or down, and two price charts (3 months and 1 year).</p><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Remember:</strong> nothing in the app is financial advice. It is a structured first filter for your own research — not an answer key.</div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 2 — Valuation", "lessons": [{"id": "pe-tal", "title": "The P/E ratio — the foundation of valuation", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine buying a fruit tree that yields 10 kg of apples a year, worth 100 kr. The seller wants 1,500 kr for the tree. You are then paying \"15 times\" the annual harvest — it takes 15 years of apple harvests (if they stay the same size) before the tree has \"paid back\" its price. The P/E ratio is exactly the same idea, with apples replaced by profit and the tree by a company.</p></div><p><strong>P/E</strong> stands for \"Price/Earnings\" — share price divided by earnings per share. A P/E of 15 means you pay 15 kronor for every krona the company earns per year. A lower P/E generally means the stock is cheaper relative to its earnings — but not always for good reasons.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Why a low P/E is not always good</h3><p>A very low P/E can mean the stock is a bargain — or that the market believes earnings will soon fall sharply (e.g. a company in a dying industry). A high P/E can mean the stock is expensive — or that the market expects strong future growth that will make today's price reasonable in hindsight. P/E alone never tells the whole story, which is exactly why the app weighs in many other factors too.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>P/E &lt; 15</td><td>+6 point bonus</td></tr><tr><td>P/E &gt; 40</td><td>−10 point deduction (P/E is also compared with the company's own history – see Valuation vs own history)</td></tr><tr><td>P/E missing</td><td>Common if the company is loss-making or very young — neither bonus nor penalty</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "pe-bransch", "title": "P/E in context — comparison with the industry", "body": "<p>A P/E of 30 sounds expensive for a bank, but can be completely normal for a fast-growing software company. Different industries have structurally different \"normal\" P/E levels — banks and engineering companies usually trade lower, tech and growth companies usually higher, because the market prices in future growth to very different degrees depending on the industry.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Comparing P/E straight across industries is a bit like comparing the price per square metre of a flat in central Stockholm with a house in the countryside. The price per square metre can differ enormously without either being \"wrong\" — they are completely different markets with different conditions.</p></div><p>In the app's detail view you therefore see P/E compared both with the global industry median (from Damodaran/NYU Stern data) and adjusted by country, so you don't have to do the industry comparison in your head.</p>"}, {"id": "peg-tal", "title": "The PEG ratio — P/E adjusted for growth", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Back to the fruit tree: a tree that already yields 10 kg of apples and is expected to keep yielding exactly 10 kg every year is different from a young tree that yields 2 kg today but is expected to yield 8 kg in a few years once it has grown. The young tree may look \"expensive\" on P/E right now (little fruit, high price tag), but if the growth materialises it was actually cheap. The PEG ratio is the tool that takes exactly that into account.</p></div><p><strong>PEG</strong> = P/E divided by expected earnings growth in percent. A PEG below 1 is usually interpreted as the stock being reasonably or even cheaply priced relative to how fast it is expected to grow — even if the P/E itself looks high.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>PEG &lt; 1</td><td>+8 points — P/E looks reasonable given expected growth</td></tr><tr><td>PEG &gt; 3</td><td>−10 points — expensive even after accounting for growth</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "forward-pe", "title": "Forward P/E — what the market expects ahead", "body": "<p>Ordinary P/E is based on the past year's earnings — already happened, already known. <strong>Forward P/E</strong> is instead based on analysts' expected earnings for the coming year. The difference between the two tells you something important: if forward P/E is much lower than today's P/E, the market expects earnings to grow substantially. If it is higher, earnings are expected to fall.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>It's the difference between asking \"how much did you earn last year?\" and \"how much do you think you'll earn next year?\". Both answers are useful, but the second says more about where things are heading.</p></div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Forward P/E &lt; −15% vs historical</td><td>+8 points — earnings growth expected</td></tr><tr><td>Forward P/E &gt; +15% vs historical</td><td>−10 points — earnings decline expected</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "pb-tal", "title": "The P/B ratio — price vs book value", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine selling your house. \"Book value\" is roughly what would be left if you sold off everything the company owns (property, machinery, cash) and paid off all debts — simply the net worth. P/B compares the share price with that net worth per share.</p></div><p>A P/B below 1 means the stock trades below what the company's assets are formally worth on paper — which is sometimes a bargain, but sometimes a warning sign that the market doubts the assets are really worth what the books say (e.g. outdated equipment, uncertain receivables).</p><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Value trap:</strong> a low P/B combined with high debt can mean that \"the cheap part\" is really just a reflection of the company being riskily indebted. The app therefore gives no bonus for a low P/B if debt is also high.</div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>P/B &lt; 0</td><td>−25 points — negative equity, serious warning sign</td></tr><tr><td>0 &lt; P/B &lt; 1.5 (without high debt)</td><td>+8 points</td></tr><tr><td>P/B &gt; 6</td><td>−5 points — weak measure for companies with few physical assets</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "utdelning", "title": "Dividends — getting paid to own", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>If the stock is a rental property, the dividend is the rental income you receive every month, on top of the property's value also being able to rise or fall. Some companies pay out part of their profit directly to shareholders, others reinvest everything in growth instead.</p></div><p><strong>Dividend yield</strong> is the dividend as a percentage of the share price — how much \"rent\" you get per invested krona per year. Young growth companies rarely pay anything at all (they would rather reinvest the money to grow faster), while mature, stable companies often pay a steady stream.</p>"}, {"id": "riskpremie", "title": "Risk-free rate and risk premium", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>You can put your money in an ordinary savings account and get a guaranteed, low rate — zero risk, zero excitement. Or you can lend money to a friend who is starting a business — higher possible return, but also the risk of never seeing the money again. You should reasonably demand MORE expected return to take that risk, otherwise the savings account is the better choice. The extra return you demand for taking the risk is the risk premium.</p></div><p>The <strong>risk-free rate</strong> is the return on the safest possible alternative — in practice a government bond yield (e.g. Swedish 10-year government bonds). The <strong>risk premium</strong> in the app is calculated as the stock's \"earnings yield\" (100 divided by P/E — the inverse of the P/E ratio) minus the risk-free rate.</p><p>If the risk premium is <strong>negative</strong>, it means that, mathematically, you get a better return completely risk-free in a safe account right now — a clear warning sign, even if the stock otherwise looks good.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Risk premium &gt; 8pp</td><td>+10 points — plenty of compensation for the risk</td></tr><tr><td>Risk premium &lt; 0</td><td>−10 points — you get more risk-free right now</td></tr></table></div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 3 — Technical analysis", "lessons": [{"id": "sma", "title": "Moving averages (SMA) — seeing the trend through the noise", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine weighing yourself every day. The weight jumps up and down a little from day to day depending on what you ate yesterday, fluid balance etc. — pure noise that doesn't say much. But if you calculate your <em>average over the last 20 days</em> and compare it with the average <em>over the last 200 days</em>, you suddenly see a clear direction: are you gaining or losing weight over time, beyond daily swings?</p></div><p>An <strong>SMA</strong> (Simple Moving Average) does exactly that with the share price. The app calculates three: <strong>SMA20</strong> (last 20 days, reacts quickly), <strong>SMA50</strong> (medium term) and <strong>SMA200</strong> (long term, slow but stable).</p><svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n          <rect width=\"640\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#17140f\"/>\n          <text x=\"20\" y=\"26\" fill=\"#756b57\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Price (grey), SMA50 (blue), SMA200 (amber)</text>\n          <polyline points=\"20,150 80,120 140,160 200,100 260,140 320,90 380,110 440,70 500,95 560,60 620,80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#756b57\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.5\"/>\n          <polyline points=\"20,155 80,148 140,142 200,132 260,124 320,112 380,104 440,92 500,86 560,76 620,72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7ba687\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n          <polyline points=\"20,165 80,163 140,160 200,158 260,155 320,152 380,148 440,144 500,140 560,136 620,132\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d9a441\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n          <circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"104\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#7ba687\"/>\n          <text x=\"390\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#7ba687\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"11\">Golden cross</text>\n        </svg><p>When the short average (SMA50) passes <strong>above</strong> the long one (SMA200), it is interpreted as the trend having just turned upwards — the short-term average \"wins\" over the long-term one. This is called a <strong>golden cross</strong>. The opposite, crossing downwards, is called a <strong>death cross</strong>.</p>"}, {"id": "golden-death-cross", "title": "Golden cross, death cross — and why they come late", "body": "<p>Golden cross and death cross are well-known, widely used signals — but they have a built-in weakness: they are <strong>lagging confirmations</strong>. Since SMA50 itself is an average of prices that have already passed, it must \"catch up\" with a trend change before the crossing even happens. By the time it occurs, the trend has often been going on for a while.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>It's a bit like judging whether spring has arrived by looking at the average temperature over the last month compared with the last six months. That signal appears after spring can already be felt, not before.</p></div><h3 class=\"sub\">The earlier variant: SMA20 vs SMA50</h3><p>That is exactly why the app also has a <strong>faster</strong> signal: SMA20 vs SMA50 instead of SMA50 vs SMA200. It reacts earlier to a trend change, but is also <strong>noisier</strong> — more false signals, since a shorter average is more sensitive to temporary swings.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>+↑ (yellow)</td><td>Golden cross on SMA50/SMA200, or within a 2% margin of happening</td></tr><tr><td>+↓ (red)</td><td>Death cross on SMA50/SMA200, or within a 2% margin</td></tr><tr><td>↑20 / ↓20</td><td>Same idea but on SMA20/SMA50 — faster, noisier, earlier warning</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "rsi", "title": "RSI — momentum and overbought/oversold levels", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Think of a rubber band. If you stretch it a lot in one direction, tension builds up that often (but not always) results in it snapping back. RSI roughly measures how \"stretched\" a stock's recent price move has been.</p></div><p><strong>RSI</strong> (Relative Strength Index) is a number between 0 and 100 that measures how strong and sustained the recent gains have been compared with the losses, over the last 14 days.</p><svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 140\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n          <rect width=\"640\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#17140f\"/>\n          <rect x=\"40\" y=\"40\" width=\"560\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#c06a45\" opacity=\"0.25\"/>\n          <rect x=\"40\" y=\"70\" width=\"280\" height=\"0\" fill=\"none\"/>\n          <rect x=\"182\" y=\"40\" width=\"276\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#2b271e\"/>\n          <rect x=\"40\" y=\"40\" width=\"142\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#7ba687\" opacity=\"0.25\"/>\n          <text x=\"45\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#7ba687\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">0–35: Oversold</text>\n          <text x=\"250\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#b2a890\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">35–70: Neutral</text>\n          <text x=\"475\" y=\"60\" fill=\"#c06a45\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">70–100: Overbought</text>\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#756b57\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n          <text x=\"40\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#756b57\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\">0</text>\n          <text x=\"590\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#756b57\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"10\">100</text>\n        </svg><p>A <strong>low RSI (below 35)</strong> suggests the stock has been sold off heavily recently and may be \"oversold\" — a possible buying opportunity if the decline was overdone. A <strong>high RSI (above 70)</strong> suggests the opposite — a strong rise that may be ripe for a pause or pullback. RSI says nothing about <em>why</em> — only that the move has been unusually strong in one direction.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>RSI &lt; 35</td><td>+10 points (buy), +14 (growth) — potentially oversold</td></tr><tr><td>RSI &gt; 70</td><td>−10 points (buy), −12 (growth) — potentially overbought</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "volym", "title": "Volume — how many are trading", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Picture a shop. On an ordinary Tuesday maybe 50 customers walk past. But one day there is an extreme crowd, a queue out onto the street — something unusual is obviously drawing people's attention. Volume measures the same thing for a stock: how many shares have changed hands during the day.</p></div><p><strong>Volume deviation</strong> compares today's trading volume with the normal average. A strong deviation (2× the average or more) means that something — news, a report, a rumour — has made significantly more people buy or sell than usual. Volume in itself doesn't say which direction, only that interest has increased sharply, which often precedes or follows a larger price move.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Volume &gt; 2× average</td><td>+7 points — a larger move may be under way</td></tr></table></div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 4 — Fundamental quality & growth", "lessons": [{"id": "intaktstillvaxt", "title": "Revenue growth — is the company growing?", "body": "<p>The most basic growth measure of all: how much more did the company sell last quarter compared with <em>the same quarter last year</em>? The comparison is made against the same period the previous year, not the previous quarter, to avoid seasonal patterns (Christmas, summer) giving a misleading picture.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>If an ice-cream kiosk sells more ice cream in July than in January, that says nothing about whether the business is doing well or badly — it just says it's summer. But if you compare this July with last July, you see the real development, free of seasonality.</p></div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Growth &gt; 15%</td><td>+8 points (standard model), +16 (growth model)</td></tr><tr><td>Growth &lt; −5%</td><td>−8 points (standard model), −12 (growth model)</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "marginaltrend", "title": "Operating margin and margin trend", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Two pizzerias can have exactly the same sales but earn completely different amounts — one may have expensive rent and waste ingredients, the other is run efficiently. <strong>Operating margin</strong> measures how much of each krona of revenue is left as profit after operating costs.</p></div><p>Even more interesting than the margin itself is the <strong>trend</strong> — is profitability improving or deteriorating over time? A company that grows AND becomes more profitable per krona of revenue shows signs of <strong>scalability</strong>: the business becomes more efficient as it grows, not just bigger.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Margin improves &gt; 3pp</td><td>+8 points (standard), +14 (growth) — sign of scalability</td></tr><tr><td>Margin deteriorates &gt; 3pp</td><td>−8 points (standard), −10 (growth)</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "fcf-marginal", "title": "Free cash flow (FCF) — real money, not just numbers on paper", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Your personal finances can look good on paper (high salary) but still leave your account shrinking every month if expenses are even higher. In the same way, a company's accounting \"profit\" can look fine while the business is actually burning money in practice — e.g. because it ties up a lot of capital in inventory or investments. <strong>Free cash flow</strong> measures the actual money left after the bills are paid and necessary investments made.</p></div><p><strong>FCF margin</strong> is free cash flow as a percentage of revenue — how much of each krona of revenue is actually left as real, spendable money. A negative FCF margin means the company consumes more cash than it brings in, which can be completely normal for a young company in a growth phase — but unsustainable in the long run if it goes on too long without a clear plan.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>FCF margin &lt; 0</td><td>−15 points (standard). Growth model: cash burn (negative FCF and/or ROIC below −10 %) is counted once, −10 — or −5 if revenue grows more than 15 %</td></tr><tr><td>FCF margin &gt; 15%</td><td>+8 points</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "roic", "title": "ROIC — return on invested capital", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine two people who each get 100,000 kr to invest in a small business of their own. After a year, one has turned the money into 120,000 kr of profit-generating assets, the other only into 102,000 kr. The first person is simply better at turning capital into value — regardless of whether the starting capital came from their own savings or a loan. ROIC measures exactly that ability in a company.</p></div><p><strong>ROIC</strong> (Return on Invested Capital) measures how efficiently the company turns all the capital it has access to — equity AND borrowed capital, minus cash — into profit. It is one of the most respected quality measures in investing, because it can't be \"faked\" simply by borrowing more money (unlike some other profitability measures).</p><p>ROIC above 15% is generally considered strong — the company creates considerably more value than the capital would cost to borrow or than the owners can reasonably demand in return. Below 6% is considered weak.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">A multi-year average, not just the latest year</h3><p>A single year can be misleading — a one-off boost or a temporary dip says little about the real ability. The app therefore calculates ROIC for each financial year Yahoo Finance provides (normally about 3–4 years, <strong>not ten</strong> — worth remembering) and uses the average. If even the worst year is above 8% it is seen as extra stable; if ROIC swings sharply between years, points are deducted instead, since stability is itself a sign of quality.</p><p>For banks, insurers and real estate companies/REITs, ROIC is shown as <strong>\"Not applicable\"</strong> — their capital structure differs so much from ordinary operating companies that the measure becomes misleading rather than informative.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>ROIC avg &gt; 15%</td><td>+5 points (standard), +8 (growth)</td></tr><tr><td>ROIC avg 10–15%</td><td>+3 points (standard)</td></tr><tr><td>ROIC avg &lt; 6%</td><td>−4 points (standard)</td></tr><tr><td>Worst year &gt; 8%</td><td>+2 points (stability bonus)</td></tr><tr><td>Large swings between years</td><td>−2 points</td></tr><tr><td>Total cap</td><td>Max ±6 points — should not dominate over e.g. the FCF margin</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "skuldsattning", "title": "Debt (D/E) — how much is borrowed?", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>If you buy a house for 3 million with a 300,000 kr down payment and the rest as a mortgage, your loan-to-value is high — a small drop in the house's value can wipe out your entire equity. A company with high debt (D/E, Debt/Equity) works the same way: a small setback can hit the owners' share of the value hard, since the loans must be repaid no matter what.</p></div><p><strong>D/E</strong> compares the company's total debt with its equity. A lower D/E means less financial leverage and therefore generally lower risk — but some borrowing is normal and even healthy for most companies, just as a reasonable mortgage is for most households.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>D/E &lt; 50</td><td>+5 points</td></tr><tr><td>D/E &gt; 150</td><td>−10 points</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "sbc", "title": "Stock-based compensation (SBC) — a hidden dilution risk", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Imagine the pizzeria's ten owners decide to pay the staff with new shares in the pizzeria instead of cash wages. The staff are happy, but the existing ten owners' share of the pizzeria shrinks — the same pizzeria is now split into more pieces. That is exactly what stock-based compensation does: the company partly \"pays\" with new shares instead of cash.</p></div><p>Stock-based compensation (SBC) is especially common in growth and tech companies as a way to attract staff without draining the cash. The problem is that it <strong>dilutes</strong> existing shareholders' stakes over time, and makes reported earnings less representative of what shareholders actually get to keep.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>SBC &gt; 15% of revenue</td><td>−10 points (standard), −8 (growth) — dilution risk</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "capex", "title": "Capex vs depreciation — the investment rate", "body": "<p><strong>Capex</strong> (Capital Expenditure) is money the company spends on new equipment, factories or infrastructure. <strong>Depreciation</strong> is the accounting cost of old equipment gradually becoming worth less. Comparing the two — capex several times higher than depreciation — means the company is investing considerably more in future capacity than is needed just to maintain the existing.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>It's the difference between just replacing a broken dishwasher (maintenance, corresponding to depreciation) and building a whole new kitchen extension (expansion, corresponding to high capex). Both cost money, but only one of the investments is meant for growth.</p></div><p>A high investment rate can be a good sign of growth ambition — but also means more uncertainty about whether the investments will actually pay off.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Capex &gt; 5× depreciation</td><td>−5 points — aggressive investment rate, more uncertainty</td></tr></table></div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 5 — Risk & quality checks", "lessons": [{"id": "borsvarde-risk", "title": "Market cap and micro-cap risk", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>A small corner shop can double its turnover in a year relatively easily — but can also close for good if the owner falls ill or the rent rises sharply. A large retail chain moves more slowly in both directions: harder to double turnover quickly, but also much harder to go under overnight. Market cap signals roughly the same thing about a company.</p></div><p>Very small companies (<strong>micro-caps</strong>) can deliver huge returns if they succeed — but often lack the financial buffer, diversification and public scrutiny that larger companies have. Technical signals (such as SMA and RSI) also become less reliable with thin trading, since a few trades can move the price a lot without it really meaning anything.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Market cap &lt; 50M USD</td><td>−25 points (standard model) — micro-cap, high risk</td></tr><tr><td>Market cap &lt; 300M USD</td><td>−10 points</td></tr><tr><td>In the Growth tab</td><td>No market cap penalty at all — small size is expected, not a flaw, in that model</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "likviditet", "title": "Liquidity — can you actually sell when you want?", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Think of selling an ordinary, popular flat in central Gothenburg compared with a unique, unusually designed house in the countryside. The flat has many potential buyers — you can sell quickly at a fair price. The unusual house may only have a handful of interested buyers in the whole country — you may have to cut the price sharply or wait a long time to find a buyer.</p></div><p><strong>Liquidity</strong> measures how easy a stock is to buy and sell without the transaction itself moving the price. Low daily turnover means that even a relatively small order from you can move the price against you — a risk that is easy to forget until you actually try to sell in a downturn.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Daily turnover &lt; 100,000 (in trading currency)</td><td>−20 points — extremely low liquidity</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "volatilitet", "title": "Volatility — how bumpy is the ride?", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Two car journeys can take exactly the same time and end at the same destination, but one runs on a smooth motorway while the other bumps along a potholed gravel road. Both arrive — but the bumpy ride is much harder to sit through, and it is easier to jump out in panic halfway, even though the destination was the same.</p></div><p><strong>Volatility</strong> measures how much the share price swings up and down over time, regardless of direction. High volatility means larger and more irregular price swings — which increases the risk that you sell in panic at the wrong moment, even if the stock would ultimately have done well had you held on.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Volatility &gt; 80% (annualised)</td><td>−15 points</td></tr><tr><td>Volatility 45–80%</td><td>−5 points</td></tr><tr><td>Volatility &lt; 20%</td><td>+5 points — stable price development</td></tr><tr><td>In the Growth tab</td><td>No effect at all — high volatility is expected and normal for young companies in that model</td></tr></table></div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 6 — External signals & the big picture", "lessons": [{"id": "analytiker", "title": "Analyst consensus and price targets", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Checking aggregated restaurant reviews before booking a table is often better than a single review — more independent judges reduce the risk of being steered by someone's bad day or odd taste. Analyst consensus works similarly: a weighting of several professional analysts' recommendations for a stock.</p></div><p>Analysts covering a company give recommendations (strong buy → buy → hold → sell → strong sell) and a <strong>price target</strong> — what they think the stock should be worth in about 12 months. The consensus is the average of all covering analysts' views.</p><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Important to remember:</strong> analysts often have systematic conflicts of interest (banks giving recommendations may also want the company as a client for other services) and have historically been poor at predicting major turning points. See it as ONE data point among many, never an answer key.</div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Strong buy</td><td>+18 points (standard) · Hold</td></tr><tr><td>Hold</td><td>−5 points — analysts see neither clear upside nor downside</td></tr><tr><td>Strong sell</td><td>−25 points</td></tr><tr><td>BUY flag downgraded</td><td>If the score qualifies for BUY but consensus says hold/sell, HOLD is shown instead — consensus can \"override\" the score in the interface</td></tr></table></div>"}, {"id": "investtech", "title": "Investtech Top 20 — an external technical signal", "body": "<p>Investtech is an established Nordic technical analysis provider that daily ranks the 20 technically strongest stocks on the Stockholm exchange, based on trend, support/resistance, patterns and volume — a medium-term horizon of 1–6 months. The app weighs in this external, independent assessment as a small bonus when a watched stock is on the list.</p><div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>It's like getting another independent expert to look at the same picture you have already analysed yourself — if your assessments point the same way it strengthens your case; if they point different ways it's worth asking why.</p></div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Rank #1–5</td><td>+10 points</td></tr><tr><td>Rank #6–10</td><td>+7 points</td></tr><tr><td>Rank #11–20</td><td>+5 points</td></tr></table></div><p>The list is updated manually, not in real time — so treat it as a periodic check rather than a live signal.</p>"}, {"id": "makropanel", "title": "The yield curve and credit spreads — what the bond market knows before the stock market", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>A stock investor asks \"will this company be worth more next quarter?\" — a question easily coloured by optimism and stories. A bond investor lending money for ten years asks a completely different question: \"will I get my money back?\". That question forces a sober assessment of inflation, the economy and whether the borrower will even survive — and historically the bond market has often priced in problems weeks or months before the stock market reacts.</p></div><p>The app monitors two such signals from the US bond market, both free from FRED (Federal Reserve):</p><h3 class=\"sub\">The yield curve (10-year yield minus 2-year yield)</h3><p>Normally longer loans pay a higher rate than short ones — you should be compensated for locking your money up longer. When this flips and short rates become higher than long ones, it is called an <strong>inversion</strong>. The bond market is then signalling that it expects worse times and lower rates ahead. A sustained inversion has preceded nearly every US recession since the 1950s.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Credit spread (high-yield)</h3><p>How much extra interest risky companies must pay compared with safe government bonds to borrow money. If that extra rate widens sharply, it means the bond market is starting to worry about bankruptcies and credit losses in the system.</p><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Important caveat, the same as shown in the app's macro panel:</strong> these are broad, slow regime signals — not a forecast for individual stocks or exact timing. The lead time to any downturn has historically ranged between 6 and 24 months, and there are false signals (e.g. 1966, when an inversion was not followed by a recession). Treat it as \"raise your guard\", not as an answer key.</div><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Yield curve inverted ≥30 days</td><td>−5 points, broad caution across all stocks</td></tr><tr><td>Credit spread &gt;5pp (elevated)</td><td>−5 points</td></tr><tr><td>Credit spread &gt;8pp (crisis level)</td><td>−10 points</td></tr></table></div><p>Unlike all other factors in this guide, the macro panel affects <strong>all stocks' scores at once</strong> with the same small adjustment, since it is a signal about the whole market climate, not about a single company. You find it under the <strong>MACRO</strong> tab at the top of the app.</p>"}, {"id": "helheten", "title": "How everything is weighed together", "body": "<p>You have now gone through all the individual building blocks. This is how they fit together:</p><svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n          <rect width=\"640\" height=\"200\" fill=\"#17140f\"/>\n          <text x=\"20\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#b2a890\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">50 (start)</text>\n          <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"60\" height=\"120\" fill=\"#2b271e\"/>\n          <text x=\"30\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#ede7d9\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"13\">50</text>\n          <text x=\"110\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#7ba687\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">+ Bonuses (dampened)</text>\n          <rect x=\"110\" y=\"70\" width=\"60\" height=\"90\" fill=\"#7ba687\" opacity=\"0.35\"/>\n          <text x=\"220\" y=\"30\" fill=\"#c06a45\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"12\">− Deductions (full)</text>\n          <rect x=\"220\" y=\"90\" width=\"60\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#c06a45\" opacity=\"0.35\"/>\n          <text x=\"330\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#756b57\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"18\">=</text>\n          <rect x=\"380\" y=\"30\" width=\"80\" height=\"130\" fill=\"#d9a441\" opacity=\"0.25\" stroke=\"#d9a441\"/>\n          <text x=\"395\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#d9a441\" font-family=\"IBM Plex Mono, monospace\" font-size=\"16\">Final score</text>\n        </svg><p><strong>Deductions always count in full</strong> — a bad stock should be able to end up near zero. <strong>Bonuses are dampened</strong> after 25 points (the first 25 count in full, the rest only at 40%) — this makes scores near 100 structurally rare, and keeps them so even as new factors are added to the model over time.</p><h3 class=\"sub\">Geopolitics and macro</h3><p>Beyond company-specific factors, the app also weighs in manually updated sector and country weights linked to real world events (e.g. the Iran conflict's effect on oil prices and the semiconductor sector) — these fall into the same dampened bonus pool, not as a separate, unlimited addition. The combined geopolitical weight per stock is capped at ±10 points.</p>"}, {"id": "kop-salj-tillvaxt", "title": "Buy, sell and growth scores — three different lenses", "body": "<p>The app actually calculates three different scores, with different purposes:</p><ul><li><strong>Buy score</strong> — the standard model, the one you have seen most examples of in this guide. Balanced between valuation, trend, quality and risk.</li><li><strong>Sell score</strong> — mirrored logic, calculated entirely in your browser for your own holdings (never sent to any server). The same factors, but interpreted from the perspective \"does this argue FOR selling?\" rather than buying.</li><li><strong>Growth score</strong> — a deliberately recalibrated model for young, hand-picked companies in the Growth tab. Micro-cap and volatility penalties are removed (small size is expected, not a flaw), while growth and momentum signals weigh considerably more. Hard risk checks such as liquidity and financial-stress combinations are unchanged, however — potential should never be allowed to outweigh genuine warning signs.</li></ul><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Congratulations, you have been through the stock part of the guide!</strong> The next step to really cement the knowledge is to open a few stocks in the app and see if you can predict why they got the score they did, before reading the list of reasons. Module 7 covers funds and the stability gauge.</div>"}]}, {"title": "Module 7 — Funds & market stability", "lessons": [{"id": "fondpoang", "title": "The fund score — how promising does a fund look right now?", "body": "<div class=\"analogy\"><div class=\"lbl\">Analogy</div><p>Choosing a fund is a bit like choosing a bus: all buses on the same route (same category) go roughly the same way, but some run on time, have a low fare and a calm driver. The fund score compares buses on the same route with each other, not a city bus with a train.</p></div><p>Each fund gets a score of 0–100 made up of five parts. Comparisons are made <strong>within the same category</strong> as far as possible — a Swedish index fund against other Swedish index funds, a bond fund against other bond funds.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>Trend (35)</td><td>How far above/below SMA200 the price is, whether SMA200 is rising or falling, price above SMA50, golden cross and RSI</td></tr><tr><td>Momentum (25)</td><td>Blended 3/6/12-month return – half ranked within the category, half against all comparable funds</td></tr><tr><td>Risk-adjusted return (25)</td><td>Sharpe ratio vs the policy rate and the largest drop over the past year</td></tr><tr><td>Fee (10)</td><td>≤0.3 % gives full points, above 1.5 % gives zero</td></tr><tr><td>Macro (5)</td><td>The stability score for bond and real estate funds, market breadth for equity funds</td></tr><tr><td>≥75 / &lt;50</td><td>Promising / weak</td></tr></table></div><h3 class=\"sub\">Category climate — tailwind or headwind for the sector</h3><p>Under <strong>All funds</strong> each category banner shows its own 0–100 climate: <strong>Tailwind</strong> (65+), <strong>Neutral</strong> (45–64) or <strong>Headwind</strong> (below 45), plus an arrow for the change over the past month. It is not measured on the funds themselves but on a market index for the category – e.g. OMXS30 for Swedish index funds, the S&amp;P 500 for the US and gold/silver for precious metal funds – using trend (40), momentum (40) and risk (20). For bond funds the stability score is used. The climate does not affect the fund score; it shows whether the whole sector has the wind at its back right now.</p><div class=\"callout-warn\"><strong>Remember:</strong> the fund score measures how the fund looks <em>right now</em> – trend and momentum turn. It says nothing about the manager's long-term skill and is not financial advice.</div>"}, {"id": "stabilitet", "title": "The stability gauge — rates and credit in one number", "body": "<p>Under <strong>MACRO → STABILITY</strong> a gauge with a needle shows how stable the rates and credit environment is, from very unstable (left) to very stable (right). The 0–100 score combines six categories: rate moves, yield curve, credit, real yield and inflation, bond volatility (MOVE), and Europe and the Nordics.</p><p>The chart below the gauge shows how the score has moved week by week or month by month. The history is calculated with exactly the same formula, using only data that existed on each date.</p><div class=\"in-app\"><div class=\"lbl\">In the app</div><table><tr><td>70–100 Stable</td><td>+2 points on all stocks at ≥80</td></tr><tr><td>50–70 Mixed</td><td>0 to −2 points</td></tr><tr><td>30–50 Unstable</td><td>−4 to −6 points</td></tr><tr><td>0–30 Stressed</td><td>−8 points</td></tr><tr><td>Trend ±5 in a month</td><td>±2 extra points</td></tr></table></div>"}]}];
window.GLOSSARY_EN = {"PRIS": {"title": "Price", "text": "Current price per share on the exchange, in the company's trading currency."}, "P/E": {"title": "P/E ratio", "text": "Price divided by earnings per share. Shows roughly how many years of earnings you \"pay for\" in the share price. A lower P/E can mean a cheaper stock – but can also mean the market expects weaker earnings ahead. Best compared with similar companies in the same industry."}, "PEG-TAL": {"title": "PEG ratio", "text": "The P/E ratio divided by expected earnings growth. Relates the price to how fast the company is expected to grow – a high P/E can be reasonable for a fast-growing company but expensive for one that barely grows at all. Below 1 is usually considered attractive, above 3 expensive. Based on analysts' growth forecasts, which, like price targets, are estimates, not facts."}, "SHARPEKVOT": {"title": "Sharpe ratio", "text": "The fund's return over the past year minus the short risk-free rate (the policy rate in the fund's currency), divided by volatility. Shows how much extra return you got per unit of risk. Above 1 is good, below 0 means the fund returned less than a risk-free investment."}, "MAX FALL 1 ÅR": {"title": "Largest drop (max drawdown)", "text": "The largest fall from a peak to a subsequent trough over the past year. Shows how painful it can be to own the fund in a downturn."}, "RANK I KATEGORI": {"title": "Momentum rank", "text": "The fund's position among comparable funds based on a blend of 3, 6 and 12-month returns. Half of the momentum score is ranked within the same category (when it has at least 3 funds), half against all comparable funds (equity vs equity, bond vs bond)."}, "SMA50 / SMA200": {"title": "SMA50 vs SMA200", "text": "Golden cross = the 50-day average is above the 200-day average, a classic signal of an upward trend. Death cross = the opposite."}, "AVSTÅND TILL SMA200": {"title": "Distance to SMA200", "text": "How far the price is above or below its 200-day average, and whether that average is rising or falling. The fund score's trend part is graded by both – further above a rising SMA200 gives more points."}, "VÄRDERING MOT EGEN HISTORIK": {"title": "Valuation vs own history", "text": "A first screen of whether the stock is cheap or expensive compared with itself. The company is assigned a phase: are sales growing, is it profitable, and does it return money through dividends or buybacks. Profitable companies are compared on P/E, loss-making ones on P/S (price/sales). Today's figure is compared with the company's median over roughly the last 4 years: more than 15 % below = CHEAP, more than 15 % above = EXPENSIVE. Gives ±3 points at 15–30 % deviation and ±6 above 30 % (halved for cyclical sectors, no bonus if earnings are expected to fall). Note that the history includes the low-rate years – with today's higher rates a somewhat lower P/E than the average is often justified."}, "FORWARD P/E": {"title": "Forward P/E", "text": "Same principle as ordinary P/E, but based on EXPECTED future earnings instead of the latest reported. The symbol shows whether forward P/E is clearly lower (▲, green – earnings growth expected) or clearly higher (▼, red – earnings decline expected) than the ordinary P/E. Based on analysts' earnings forecasts, not facts."}, "RISKFRI RÄNTA": {"title": "Risk-free rate", "text": "What a 10-year government bond in the company's home country yields right now – the return you can get with practically no risk. The reference point everything else is measured against: why take stock risk for less than that? Fetched automatically on every run from FRED (the US daily, other countries from OECD monthly data, which lags by a month or so)."}, "RISKPREMIE": {"title": "Risk premium", "text": "The stock's earnings yield (100/P/E) minus the risk-free rate – how much EXTRA return the stock offers for the risk you take compared with a safe investment. A negative risk premium (▼, red) means you get a better return completely risk-free right now, which is a warning sign. A high risk premium (▲, green) means plenty of compensation for the risk – but it is based on today's earnings, not a guarantee about the future."}, "ROIC (FLERÅRSSNITT)": {"title": "ROIC – return on invested capital (multi-year average)", "text": "How efficiently the company turns capital – equity and borrowed, minus cash – into profit, regardless of how it is financed. One of the clearest quality measures for separating genuinely good companies from mediocre ones. Calculated as an average over the financial years Yahoo Finance provides, normally about 3–4 years (not ten) – requires at least 3 years to be shown at all. An average above 15% is considered strong, below 6% weak. A stable worst year above 8% gives an extra plus; large swings between years give a deduction. Shown as \"Not applicable\" for banks, insurers and real estate companies/REITs, whose capital structure makes the measure misleading. Calculated from the income statement and balance sheet; may be missing for smaller/foreign companies."}, "INTÄKTSTILLVÄXT (YOY)": {"title": "Revenue growth (year over year)", "text": "How much the company's revenue grew in the latest quarter compared with the same quarter last year (avoids seasonal effects, e.g. Christmas trading always being stronger than summer). Separates companies that grow consistently from companies that just happen to look cheap right now."}, "MARGINALTREND": {"title": "Margin trend", "text": "The change in operating margin (operating profit/revenue) in the latest quarter compared with the same quarter last year, in percentage points. An improved margin means the company has become more profitable per krona of revenue – not just growing, but growing with better profitability."}, "RSI14": {"title": "RSI14 (momentum)", "text": "Measures whether a stock has traded sharply up or down recently, on a scale of 0–100. Above 70 is often called \"overbought\" (may be ripe for a pause), below 30 \"oversold\". Says nothing about whether the company is good or bad – only whether the price has moved a lot recently."}, "SMA20 / SMA50 / SMA200": {"title": "SMA20 / SMA50 / SMA200 (moving averages)", "text": "Average price over the last 20, 50 and 200 days respectively, smoothing out daily swings to show the underlying trend. The arrows show the direction: SMA20 vs SMA50 gives a faster but noisier early signal, SMA50 vs SMA200 gives the slower but more reliable golden/death cross signal."}, "VOLYM": {"title": "Volume", "text": "How much the stock traded on the latest day, compared with the average over the last 20 days. Sharply elevated volume can mean something important has happened to the company – news, a report, or large buying/selling by individual players."}, "KURSMÅL": {"title": "Price target", "text": "Average price target from the analysts covering the stock – their estimate of where the price \"should\" be in about 12 months. A collective guess, not a guarantee or an answer key."}, "ANALYTIKER": {"title": "Analyst consensus", "text": "The combined recommendation from banks/brokers currently covering the stock: strong buy, buy, hold, sell or strong sell."}, "ANTAL ANALYTIKER": {"title": "Number of analysts", "text": "How many analysts cover the stock and contribute to the consensus rating above. More analysts usually give a more reliable overall picture; fewer make individual opinions more decisive."}, "P/B": {"title": "P/B ratio", "text": "Price divided by book value (assets minus liabilities, according to the accounts). Below 1 can mean the stock trades cheaper than what is \"on the books\" – but can also be a sign that the market doesn't trust the book values."}, "UTDELNING": {"title": "Dividend", "text": "Annual dividend as a share of the share price. A high dividend can be attractive if you want ongoing income, but an unusually high figure can sometimes signal that the market expects the dividend to be cut."}, "SKULDSÄTTNING (D/E)": {"title": "Debt (D/E)", "text": "Debt relative to equity. A higher figure means the company has borrowed more relative to what the owners have put in, which increases risk – especially if rates rise or revenue falls."}, "VOLATILITET (ÅR)": {"title": "Volatility", "text": "How much the price has swung up and down recently, converted to an annual rate. Higher volatility means larger and more irregular price moves – higher risk on both the upside and the downside."}, "BETA": {"title": "Beta", "text": "How much the stock has historically swung compared with the market as a whole. Beta 1 = moves roughly like the market. Above 1 = moves more (higher risk). Below 1 = moves less."}, "FCF-MARGINAL": {"title": "FCF margin", "text": "Free cash flow (money left over after investments) as a share of revenue. A positive and high margin means the company actually generates cash, not just accounting profit on paper."}, "SBC/INTÄKTER": {"title": "Stock-based compensation / revenue", "text": "Shares and options given to employees, as a share of revenue. A high share dilutes existing shareholders' ownership over time, even though it doesn't show up as a cost in the cash flow."}, "CAPEX/AVSKRIVNINGAR": {"title": "Capex / depreciation", "text": "Investment in equipment and facilities compared with how much is depreciated in the same period. A high value means the company is investing aggressively for future growth – but it is uncertain whether it will pay off."}, "DAGAR MED SIGNAL": {"title": "Days with signal", "text": "How many runs in a row the stock has had an active buy or sell signal (score above the threshold). More days in a row may indicate a more stable signal rather than a temporary price move."}, "FÖRÄNDRING SEDAN SENAST": {"title": "Change since last", "text": "How much the score has changed since the previous run. Shows whether something new – news, a price move, a changed analyst view – has just moved the assessment in some direction."}};
